The '''[[Tesshin-shinkō|Cult of Iron]]''' ({{nihongo|Tesshin-shinkō|鉄身信仰|Cult of Iron}}) developed the mythology of the Iron Cycle into a comprehensive religious and social system. Drawing on the imagery of the forge, the Tesshin-shinkō teaches that the human body is itself a kind of smithy — a place where the soft silt of human weakness is pounded into the hard iron of discipline, strength, and resolve. The central practice is the ''tanren'' ({{nihongo|tanren|鍛錬|forging discipline}}), a system of physical training modeled on the work of the smith: just as the smith heats iron, strikes it on the anvil, quenches it in water, and strikes it again, the practitioner subjects the body to cycles of exertion, rest, and renewed exertion. The institutional successor, the [[National Iron Body Initiative]] (NIBI), maintains the Iron Body Centers ({{nihongo|Tesshin-kan|鉄身館}}) — designed to recall the forge, with iron and wood interiors arranged around a central anvil stone ({{nihongo|kinutaiwa|砧石}}) that echoes the sacred ''inoko'' stone.

